I can't access the CoreLogic contract at the moment, but my initial reactio=
n is that I don't need to be coauthor.  It allows my research assistants to=
 use the data.  However, if it also says that it's strictly for my work, th=
en I would need to be coauthor.  I'll have to read the contract carefully w=
hen I can and maybe ask Eunjee and Gary's opinion.

Attached are my comments.  You can see I struggled with Adobe commenting bu=
t eventually resorted to posting comments rather than typing text boxes.

This paper is mostly descriptive, but in a way that it could be sold as ope=
ning up a new line of research, that much of the research that has been don=
e on market transactions could also be done with the other 1/4th of transac=
tions:

  1.
Are houses that circulate through families under-utilized, mismanaged, or k=
ept vacant compared to price-allocated houses? If tax incentives or sentime=
ntal attachment causes heirs to hold onto properties they don't fully utili=
ze, it represents a spatial misalocation of housing supply in supply-constr=
ained urban areas. (2) Does the family channel slow down racial and socioec=
onomic integration?  Do in-family transfers insulate wealthy or historicall=
y segregated neighborhoods from market-driven turnover?  (3) I'm also think=
ing about rural areas where the family house is the main source of wealth i=
n an area where there are few job opportunities.  Maybe logically the effic=
ient thing to do is abandon the house and move to where the jobs are, but t=
here could be a way to disentangle the motives for staying.




