Professor Brasington,
As promised, I am sharing my current draft of the "family transfers paper" =
a.k.a paper that uses non-arms length transactions, titled, Keeping the Hou=
se in the Family: Non-Market Transfers and the Supply of American Homes.
The paper uses our CoreLogic owner-transfer deeds data to answer three main=
 questions:

  1.
How large is the family-transfer channel in the U.S. housing market? I use =
the deed records to separate arm=92s-length market sales from intra-family =
transfers, trust retitling, same-person retitling, and other non-market con=
veyances.
  2.
What happens to homes after they pass within a family? I follow parcels aft=
er family transfers to see whether they return to the open market or remain=
 outside the market for long periods.
  3.
Does tax policy affect whether inherited or family-transferred homes stay i=
n the family and prevent them from reaching the market? Answer so far is ye=
s. I use California=92s Proposition 19 as a natural experiment, since it sh=
arply limited the inherited property-tax assessment benefit created by Prop=
ositions 13 and 58 for non-occupant heirs. I have a simple framework that m=
akes predictions on family transfer homes that (if they) re-enter the marke=
t, and I show how this mechanism plays out when the tax wedge/benefit is ki=
lled.

I hope this will be an enjoyable read for you =97 I've been enjoying workin=
g on it as it is very relatable. I would be grateful for your feedback on t=
he paper, both on the big-picture framing and on the empirical strategy. I =
would also really appreciate your thoughts on whether this project is worth=
 continuing to push toward publication. It is currently missing a punchy in=
troduction which I can work on next, but the research questions, data, cont=
ribution, main findings and conclusion, are there in the draft.
One thing I wanted to clarify with you: my understanding is that, because t=
his paper uses our CoreLogic data, under the CoreLogic contract, I need you=
 to be a co-author in order to publish it. Is that correct? If so, would yo=
u be interested in joining the paper as a co-author? I would be very happy =
to add you as a co-author to the project + discuss what that would look lik=
e and revise the paper based on your guidance.

Sidenote: I will share with you work on roads and employment/wages dataset =
soon.
Thank you,
Best,

